/vertical sscale up : adding more resoures(cpu,ram) to an existing singel machine 
  Benefits: 
     Simpler to implement (no need to rewrite applications)
     Often faster to perform.
  limitations :
      There's a hardware limit to how much you can scale up.
     Can create a single point of failure (if the machine crashes, the service stops).
      costly on long run 

/horizontal scale up 
     Definition: Adding more machines or server racks to distribute the load.

    Example: Adding more web servers behind a load balancer
    Benefits:
    No limit to scalability (just add more machines).
    Increases fault tolerance (if one machine fails, others can take over).
    Limitations:
    More complex (requires changes in application, load balancing, data synchronization).
    Often more expensive and time-consuming to set up initially.

. Vertical scaling is usually easier and faster to implement, but it can become more expensive in the long run due to hardware limits.
🔹 Horizontal scaling offers better long-term scalability and reliability, but it is more complex and costlier initially.
